I'll produce answer as final text.Below is one possible goal‐model decomposition for the described multi‐robot resource‐collection–delivery mission. In the following, every goal and task is given a unique identifier, a short title, a human‐readable text, a runtime annotation (using “#” for parallel, “;” for sequential, or FALLBACK(…)) and a relation type (AND or OR, or fallback). I also provide a justification for each choice. (Note: This is only one design approach; alternative decompositions may be valid as long as they satisfy the mission’s requirements.)

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Overall Mission Structure

• The mission has two main phases:
 – Collection: The robot navigates to storage(s), sends a resource request, and waits for retrieval.
 – Delivery: The robot navigates to the delivery location and makes the required runs.
• In addition, each phase includes a battery‐check fallback:
 – In collection, if the battery falls below 10% the robot returns to recharge.
 – In delivery, if the battery falls below 30% the robot returns the resource to a checkpoint and reassigns the remaining delivery.
• Because both phases must be performed (with one branch selected based on battery condition), the overall mission goal is an Achieve goal whose target is “all required resources are collected and delivered.”

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Decomposing the Mission into Goals

We create the following hierarchy:

• G1 – Complete Mission  
  • G2 – Resource Collection  
    • G2.1 – Navigate to Storage Locations  
    • G2.2 – Request and Retrieve Resources  
    • G2.3 – Battery Check and Recharge Fallback  
 • G3 – Resource Delivery  
   • G3.1 – Normal Delivery  
   • G3.2 – Battery Check and Checkpoint Fallback

Within the collection phase the normal process (G2.1 and G2.2) is the default. However, if the battery falls below 10% then the fallback (G2.3) is activated. Similarly, in the delivery phase the normal delivery (G3.1) is attempted unless the battery is low (in which case G3.2 is used).

The runtime annotation for G1 is sequential (”;”); that is, collection must be completed before delivery. For G2 and G3 we use a fallback operator since one alternative (normal operation) is preferred but a backup (battery fallback) is triggered when needed.

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Decomposing Goals into Tasks

Each non‐leaf goal is further decomposed into tasks. For example, G2.1 (Navigate to Storage Locations) may be executed in parallel if multiple storage locations are involved; tasks in G2.2 (Request and Retrieve Resources) are executed sequentially; and the fallback in G2.3 is a sequential chain of actions. Similarly, in the delivery phase, G3.1 is sequential (navigation then delivery) while G3.2 is a sequential chain of fallback actions.

