Logical Relationships Summary

• G1 → G2 ; G3  
 – Relation: Sequential (AND)  
 – Justification: The mission is only complete when both collection (G2) and delivery (G3) phases have been successfully executed.

• G2 → FALLBACK( G2.1;G2.2, G2.3 )  
 – Relation: Fallback  
 – Justification: Normal collection (G2.1 and G2.2) is the default; however, if the battery falls below 10%, the fallback (G2.3) is activated.

• G2.1 → AT1 # AT2  
 – Relation: Parallel  
 – Justification: Navigation to different storage locations can be executed concurrently if multiple robots or storages are involved.

• G2.2 → AT3;AT4  
 – Relation: Sequential  
 – Justification: The robot must first send the resource request (AT3) and then wait for retrieval (AT4).

• G2.3 → AT5;AT6;AT7  
 – Relation: Sequential  
 – Justification: Battery monitoring and subsequent recharging/reassignment actions are performed in a fixed order.

• G3 → FALLBACK( G3.1, G3.2 )  
 – Relation: Fallback  
 – Justification: Normal delivery (G3.1) is used unless the battery falls below 30%, in which case fallback (G3.2) is activated.

• G3.1 → AT8;AT9  
 – Relation: Sequential  
 – Justification: The delivery phase involves first navigating to the location (AT8) and then delivering the resources (AT9).

• G3.2 → AT10;AT11;AT12;AT13  
 – Relation: Sequential  
 – Justification: The fallback in the delivery phase requires sequential steps to monitor battery, return the resource, reassign the task, and alert if necessary.

──────────────────────────────
Final Note

This decomposition satisfies the mission requirements by:
 • Clearly separating the collection and delivery phases.
 • Incorporating battery‐based fallbacks to handle low‐battery situations.
 • Allowing for parallel execution where multiple storage locations or robots are available.
 • Using a hierarchical tree structure (with fallback and sequential/parallel operators) that reflects the mission’s constraints and objectives.

This design is one interpretation based on the mission description provided. Adjustments (such as the number of robots or additional environmental constraints) may be applied based on further requirements or operational context.
